(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-12-01"] = {"1.1":{"s":"Matter is found as solid, liquid or gas. In a solid the particles are held in fixed positions and can only vibrate, so a solid has a fixed shape and volume. This chapter asks how the particles are arranged, how tightly they pack, what defects a real crystal has, and how arrangement decides electrical and magnetic behaviour."},"1.2":{"s":"Crystalline solids have their particles arranged in a regular pattern that repeats in three dimensions; they melt sharply at one temperature and give clean cleavage. Amorphous solids, like glass and rubber, have only short-range order and soften over a range of temperature; they are really supercooled liquids. Isomorphous substances have the same crystal shape with different chemicals; a polymorphic substance has more than one crystal form.","w":["A crystalline solid has a sharp melting point; an amorphous solid softens over a range."]},"1.3":{"s":"Crystalline solids are grouped by the particles and forces in them. Ionic crystals have ions held by electrostatic attraction (hard, brittle, conduct when molten). Covalent network crystals, such as diamond and quartz, are very hard with very high melting points. Molecular crystals have molecules held by weak forces (soft, low melting point). Metallic crystals have cations in a sea of electrons (conduct, malleable).","w":["Ionic solids do not conduct as solids, only when molten or dissolved. Metals conduct in the solid state."]},"1.4":{"s":"A lattice is an array of points; attaching the basis (a particle or a group of particles) to every point gives the crystal. The smallest repeating part of it is the unit cell. A unit cell can be primitive (particles at corners only), body-centred, face-centred or base-centred. Only fourteen different lattices, the Bravais lattices, are possible. They fall into seven crystal systems: cubic, tetragonal, orthorhombic, monoclinic, triclinic, rhombohedral and hexagonal.","w":["There are 7 crystal systems but 14 lattices; the cubic system has three of them (simple, body-centred, face-centred)."]},"1.5":{"s":"The cubic system has three unit cells: simple (particles at the eight corners), body-centred (corners and the centre) and face-centred (corners and the centre of each face). A corner particle is shared by eight cells and contributes one eighth, a face particle is shared by two cells and contributes one half, and a body-centre particle belongs wholly to its cell.","f":[{"x":"n = 8*(1/8) = 1 (sc), n = 8*(1/8) + 1 = 2 (bcc), n = 8*(1/8) + 6*(1/2) = 4 (fcc)","n":"Particles per unit cell: corners count 1/8 each, face centres 1/2 each, the body centre 1.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 1.5, p.8"}],"w":["Count the share of each particle: corner 1/8, face 1/2, edge 1/4, inside 1."]},"1.6":{"s":"Particles are packed as close as possible. In two dimensions, a square arrangement has 4 neighbours and a hexagonal arrangement 6. Stacking hexagonal layers as ABAB… gives hexagonal close packing (hcp); stacking as ABCABC… gives cubic close packing (ccp), the same as fcc. In both, each sphere touches 12 others. For N particles there are N octahedral voids and 2N tetrahedral voids.","f":[{"x":"octahedral voids = N, tetrahedral voids = 2*N","n":"In hcp and ccp, for N particles in the packing.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 1.6, p.11"}],"w":["Coordination number is 12 in both hcp and ccp, but the stacking is ABAB… in hcp and ABCABC… in ccp."]},"1.7":{"s":"Packing efficiency is the percentage of the unit cell volume occupied by the particles. It is found from the volume of the particles in one cell divided by the volume a³ of the cell. It is 52.4% for simple cubic, 68% for body-centred cubic and 74% for face-centred cubic and hcp. The density of a crystal follows from the particles per cell, the molar mass and the edge length.","f":[{"x":"Packing efficiency = (volume occupied by particles in a unit cell)/(volume of the unit cell) × 100","n":"The definition.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 1.7, p.11"},{"x":"a = 2*r; efficiency = 52.4 %","n":"Simple cubic: the particles touch along the edge of the cell.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 1.7, p.11"},{"x":"r = sqrt(3)*a/4; efficiency = 68 %","n":"Body-centred cubic: the particles touch along the body diagonal.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 1.7, p.12"},{"x":"r = a/(2*sqrt(2)); efficiency = 74 %","n":"Face-centred cubic and ccp: the particles touch along the face diagonal.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 1.7, p.13"},{"x":"rho = (n/a^3)*(M/N_A)","n":"Density of a crystal: n = particles per unit cell, a = edge length, M = molar mass, N_A = Avogadro constant.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 1.7, p.14"}],"w":["The face-diagonal is 4r in fcc and the body-diagonal is 4r in bcc: that is where r = a/(2√2) and r = √3a/4 come from.","Convert the edge length to cm (1 pm = 10⁻¹⁰ cm) before using it in the density."]},"1.8":{"s":"Real crystals have defects. In a vacancy defect a particle is missing from its site and the density falls. In a self-interstitial defect an extra particle enters the lattice and the density rises; if a lattice particle only shifts to an interstitial site, the density is unchanged. A Schottky defect (NaCl) is a missing cation-anion pair: density falls. A Frenkel defect (ZnS) is a cation moved to an interstitial site: density unchanged.","w":["Schottky defect: density decreases. Frenkel defect: density unchanged. Both keep the crystal electrically neutral.","Frenkel defects need a small cation and a large anion, so they are not found in the alkali metal halides."]},"1.9":{"s":"Solids differ greatly in electrical conductivity. In the band theory, the overlap of many atomic orbitals gives a valence band and a conduction band. In a conductor the bands overlap or the conduction band is partly filled. In an insulator a large forbidden gap separates a full valence band from an empty conduction band. In a semiconductor the gap is small. Doping a semiconductor with a group 15 element gives n-type, with a group 13 element p-type.","w":["Metals conduct less when heated; semiconductors conduct more, because heat lifts electrons across the small gap."]},"1.10":{"s":"Solids respond to a magnetic field in different ways. Diamagnetic substances have all their electrons paired and are weakly repelled. Paramagnetic substances have unpaired electrons and are weakly attracted. In a ferromagnetic substance the unpaired electrons in a region align in the same direction and the substance is strongly attracted and can be magnetised."}};
